+# Office rotation: adversarial measurement of the "rotation is costly" premise
+
+**Front:** Executive Innovation Office — adversarial / measurement front (research only; no deployment, no production changes, no bills, no Judiciary investigation).
+**Date:** 2026-11-19. **Worktree:** `office-rotation-skeptic-20261119`. All paths below are read-only observations unless stated.
+
+## Verdict (strongest case I can honestly make)
+
+Frequent rotation is **real but bounded** in cost, and the baseline overstates it in three ways. In the 95-rotation window the executive criterion that actually fires is **context tokens, not prompts** (all 55 rotated executives ended at ≥120,108 context tokens; median prompts at rotation = 3; 11 rotated after a single prompt). About **93% of those context tokens are cache reads**, so the "105K tokens per pass" figure conflates cheap cached tokens with fresh input. The largest measured waste in this system — **2,436 gate skips vs 306 dispatches** over ~50 h — was already addressed by `dispatch-gate.sh`, not by rotation. The fixed prompt payload is **~18 KB inlined** (Constitution 4,362 B + `AGENT-REPORTING-POLICY.md` 13,783 B), not the ~124 KB implied by the baseline; `LAWS.md` is referenced but was not read by the sampled sessions.
+
+Against that, **re-derivation is demonstrably real**: consecutive same-office sessions share **mean Jaccard 0.80** of investigation markers (51/54 pairs share ≥5 of 12), and the thin note **did** drop a concrete in-flight fact (a board disclosure already posted) — the successor recovered it only by re-reading the authoritative board. So my conclusion is *not* "rotation is free"; it is **"rotation's marginal cost is smaller than claimed, its benefits are mostly context-bounding, and the claimed staleness/forced-durability benefits are unverified or contradicted."**
+
+## Method and sources
+
+- `government/handover.sh` (write lines 75–90; due lines 26–46), `government/executive-cycle.sh` (prompt 74–180, policy append 198–203), `government/dispatch-gate.sh`, `government/office-continuity.sh`.
+- `state/handover/rotations.log` (95 rows), `state/handover/*.md`, `state/dispatch-gate/events.tsv` (2,743 rows, 2026-11-17 00:21Z → 2026-11-19 01:57Z), `state/liveness/reset-sequence-executive`, `state/liveness/failures/events.tsv`.
+- Read-only `antonina agent status/log` on **all 55** executive predecessor IDs in `rotations.log`; full logs for the `atLanta → levied` handover and the `dollar → glass` pair. All predecessor IDs are now `state=clearexit`.
+
+Baseline independently checked: 95 rotations, 55 executive, span 50.5 h, median executive gap **30.09 min** (baseline said 30.3), **15** executive gaps <20 min (baseline: 15), min gap 8.0 min, max 279.9 min. Rotation note = generic header + last 3 text reports truncated at 900 chars each — confirmed at `handover.sh:88-89`.
+
+## Q1 — Is rotation actually costly?
+
+**Evidence FOR cost (re-derivation):**
+- Of 55 consecutive executive pairs, 54 have both readable logs; **mean Jaccard of 12 investigation markers = 0.804, median 0.833; 51/54 pairs share ≥5 markers.** Markers include `#301 board`, running-agent list, `frontier-ledger.json`, `wander/output.log`, `antonina-interface.md`, `executive-agenda.md`, CLI `--help`, handoff queue, proceed requests.
+- Concrete: `qoDbCM…-dollar` (19 steps, 35 tool calls) and successor `E11fll…-glass` (48 tool calls) both read the same two policy files, listed running agents and open issues, fetched #301, checked the same research agent, read the same ledger, and both re-ran `antonina --help`. The fresh `ogoOPg…-levied` did the same (62 tool calls), including re-reading `handoff-queue.sh` source to re-learn its usage. That is re-derivation of static material.
+- 93% cache-read share does **not** erase this: cache reads still require a model step and still consume wall-clock and output budget.
+
+**Evidence AGAINST cost (or that it is necessary):**
+- All **55 first text reports are distinct** post-`dispatch-gate`; the "175 of 223 restated the same recovery note" pathology is gone in this window. Rotation is not producing report boilerplate.
+- Most repeated markers are **live state** (#301 messages, running agents, ledger status, sweep outputs) that genuinely changed between sessions — e.g. `dollar` posted a #301 addendum; `glass` found new sweep output; `levied` found the disclosure already landed. Re-reading those is verification, not idle re-derivation.
+- Real per-session work occurred: `dollar` posted an executive assessment; `atlanta` posted the missing disclosure; `levied` verified fronts and took no duplicate action.
+
+**Conclusion Q1:** re-derivation cost is real but concentrated in a small static set (two policy files, CLI help, directory listings). Repeated live-state reads are largely justified. I cannot attribute a token or time cost to re-derivation alone without a counterfactual replay.
+
+## Q2 — Does the thin note lose in-flight work?
+
+**Concrete case (verified).** `state/handover/executive.md` (rotated 01:41:43Z) truncates predecessor `oBh11u…-atlanta`'s final report at `“I did not steer, stop o [...]”`. The **full** report in `antonina agent log --id oBh11u…-atlanta` continues with two material facts: (a) "two research fronts registered… agents `…-scoundrel`, `…-ladies` running"; and (b) **"Only unfinished work — completed: the missing board disclosure. Posted exactly one new #301 comment … 2026-11-19T01:39:21Z, `sha256:xE_LT62f2OjG-si7xIasUs_vmHuoPH9MuW7fc6VI1IQ`."** The truncated note shows the predecessor saying it *will* post — which, read alone, invites a duplicate post, exactly what the note warns against.
+
+**It did not cause harm here, for a checkable reason.** Successor `ogoOPg…-levied`'s log says: *"Let me … confirm whether the predecessor's disclosure was posted"*, then *"The predecessor's disclosure was posted (01:39:21Z). Two research fronts are running. I won't duplicate or re-dispatch them."* The durable board state, not the note, was the operative authority. This is the mechanism `handover.sh` claims (Constitution VIII: durable records are memory).
+
+**Frequency bound:** 11 of 13 current notes contain a `[...]` truncation; truncation is routine, not exceptional. `economy.md`'s predecessor likewise flagged only "timestamp accuracy in the durable notes" as unfinished — i.e. durable-record-first discipline had already captured the substantive work.
+
+**Conclusion Q2:** thin-note loss of in-flight detail is **real and recurring**, but in the one case I could trace it was caught by mandatory live-state verification. "Durable-record-first makes it rare" is supported for *material* work; it does not make the note lossless. Counterfactual: if the board fetch had failed and the successor trusted the note, a duplicate post was plausible — unverified, but not implausible.
+
+## Q3 — Strongest case that rotation is near-free / beneficial, and that costs lie elsewhere
+
+1. **Bounds context growth — verified.** All 55 rotations occurred at last-step context 120,108–208,348 tokens, median 129,430. None ran away. The only guard against unbounded growth is this threshold; the no-rotation baseline has none.
+2. **Breaks correlated staleness — contradicted here.** Jaccard 0.80 says consecutive sessions are *more* correlated in what they inspect, not less. Rotation did not decorrelate investigations in this window. I cannot measure "staleness" directly.
+3. **Forces durable recording — plausible, unverified.** The note instructs successors to verify from records; `levied` did. But I found no experiment showing that rotation *caused* a report to be written that otherwise would not have been. The dispatch gate, not rotation, is what stopped repeated restatement.
+4. **Real costs lie elsewhere — partly verified.**
+   - **Fixed payload is smaller than baseline claims.** `executive-cycle.sh` inlines only the charter (4,362 B) and `AGENT-REPORTING-POLICY.md` (13,783 B) — ~18 KB total; `LAWS.md`, `EXECUTIVE-INNOVATION.md`, `INNOVATION-LEGISLATURE-DIALOGUE.md` are *not* inlined, and sampled sessions did not read `LAWS.md` (its appearances in logs are from an `ls` listing). The ~110 KB figure is not a per-session cost in this window.
+   - **Dominant measured waste is the already-gated no-op passes:** 2,436 skips vs 306 dispatches (clerk 668/49, executive 394/193, judiciary 508/29, recovery-steward 866/30). Rotation is not where the wasted model calls were.
+   - **`reset-sequence-executive = 197` is a stale artifact, not a current cost.** Its mtime is **2026-11-03 22:46Z** and `failures/events.tsv` stops at 2026-11-03 — the Act 19 failure-count path (`office-continuity.sh:11` threshold 3, window 30 min) was dormant throughout the 2026-11-16→19 rotation window. Adding 197 failures to 95 rotations would be a category error.
+
+## Q4 — Baselines: no rotation; tuned thresholds
+
+- **No rotation (ever-growing context):** the only evidence I found is the rationale comment at `handover.sh:19-21` — one Clerk conversation reached 2,966 prompts, median 152K and max 892K context tokens per step. That conversation is gone; **I cannot independently verify the numbers.** It is a plausible motivation, not measured evidence here. Note the two symptoms (892K context; 175 repeated notes) have *different* fixes: context is bounded by rotation, repetition by `dispatch-gate.sh`.
+- **Tuned thresholds:** `VICHE_ROTATE_PROMPTS=150` is effectively a dead letter — median prompts at rotation = 3, distribution `{1:11, 2:7, 3:16, 4:4, 5:8, 6:3, 7:1, 8:1, 9:2, 18:1, 231:1}`. Only one executive ever reached 150 prompts, and it also exceeded the context threshold. **All 55 rotations were caused by `VICHE_ROTATE_CONTEXT_TOKENS=120000`.** The context threshold binds tightly: median overshoot 9,430 tokens (7.9%), minimum 108 tokens. There is **no evidence the threshold is "wrong" in the sense of firing spuriously** — it fires exactly when crossed. Whether 120K is too *low* is untested: raising it would extend sessions, but I cannot quantify how many rotations would be avoided without replaying step-level context series. One discontinuity in `rotations.log` (`eqwQ6…-slay` at 01:30:20 is not the predecessor of the 01:41:43 rotation; `oBh11u…-atlanta` appears without a logged predecessor) shows the log is not a perfect chain — a limit on gap-based inference.
+
+## Falsifiers for my own conclusion
+
+- **"Re-derivation is bounded, not dominant":** falsified if a session-level replay shows most steps are spent re-reading static files/help rather than genuinely changed live state (my marker proxy is coarse and cannot distinguish read duration).
+- "**Note loss is caught by discipline":** falsified by any observed duplicate external action following a truncated handover, or by a successor that trusts the note without a live check.
+- "**Fixed payload is ~18 KB":** falsified if the runner injects additional large context outside `executive-cycle.sh` (e.g. a 110 KB system prompt from OpenCode config) — I could only observe step_finish token totals, not the raw system prompt.
+- "**Prompt threshold is a dead letter":** falsified by any office (not just executive) where ≥150 prompts is the binding trigger; I sampled only executive.
+- "**Rotation bounds context":** falsified if any agent exceeds the threshold without rotating (e.g. an unsafe hold or gate failure leaves a session running at >120K).
+
+## Limits
+
+Observational, single 50.5 h window, executive-heavy; no counterfactual replay, no controlled A/B, no cost/pricing data (cache-read vs input billing assumed cheaper, not priced). Log analyses use `--lines 400/3000`; long sessions' earliest steps may be missing. Agent `prompts` semantics were inferred from status JSON, not documented. "Distinct first report" is a string-equality proxy for non-repetition. No production file was modified.
+
